# Vertical Monitor Rail, Revision 2

A rail-guided riser that lets a 27" monitor slide up for use and down out of the way. This revision keeps the original concept (MGN12 linear rail plus manual clamp) and fixes the safety, mounting and fit problems found in review.

> **Status:** Not yet tested. Prices and part specifications are estimates. Check every dimension and rating against the datasheet of the parts you actually buy before cutting or drilling anything.

---

## 1. Design basis

| Parameter | Value | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Max monitor mass | **6 kg** (59 N) | Weigh your monitor with the stand removed. Above 6 kg, or for ultrawide or curved panels, re-do the checks. |
| Centre of gravity offset from rail face | ~100 mm | Rail face to VESA plate, plus about half the monitor depth. |
| Pitching moment | ~6 N·m | 59 N x 0.1 m. MGN12H blocks are rated for tens of N·m each. Confirm on the seller's datasheet. |
| Anchor design load | **3 x static load**, plus a 100 N sideways or leaning load | Covers bumps, leaning on the monitor and pulling a cable. |
| Required travel | ≥ 420 mm | About the height of a 27" panel with bezel, so it can drop below the desk line. |
| Required clamp holding force | **≥ 3 x monitor weight** (≥ 180 N at 6 kg) | Take this from the listing. If none is stated, treat the clamp as unproven and rely on the pin lock. |

**Locking philosophy**

1. **Primary lock:** a spring-loaded indexing pin that engages a hole at the "up" and "down" positions. This is a positive mechanical lock and does not rely on friction.
2. **Secondary lock:** the manual rail clamp, which stops rattle and fine-height drift.
3. **Last resort:** end stops and a bottom bumper, so a failure ends in a soft stop rather than a crash.

