## 4. Structural layout

```
 [ WALL or DESK REAR ]
         |   (anchor: §3, 3 x load)
 [ 18 mm BIRCH PLY SPINE ]  -- flat, straight, not warped
         |   (M3 bolts into tee-nuts, every hole)
 [ MGN12 RAIL, 600 mm ]  <-- top end stop + top bumper
         |
 [ BLOCK 1 ] + [ CLAMP ]   [ BLOCK 2 ]   (about 100 mm apart)
         |
 [ ALUMINIUM ADAPTER PLATE ]  -- carries indexing pin
         |                       (VESA pattern drilled in)
 [ MONITOR ]
         |
 [ BOTTOM BUMPER + BOTTOM END STOP ]

 Beside the rail: [ PIN-HOLE STRIP ]  holes at "UP" and "DOWN"
```

### 4.1 Spine

- 18 mm birch plywood, about 650 x 120 mm. Check it is flat along its length with a straight edge, and reject warped boards. A twisted spine will bind the carriage.
- Pre-drill and fit M3 tee-nuts or threaded inserts on the rear face at every rail hole position.

### 4.2 Rail

- Use the rail itself as a drilling template: centre it on the spine, mark every hole, drill 3.2-3.5 mm holes, then fit tee-nuts or inserts.
- Fix with **M3 x 16 socket-head cap screws in every hole**. Do not use tapered-head wood screws, because the counterbores won't seat them and a proud head will catch the block.
- Tighten from one end to the other and keep the rail straight. Check the block runs smoothly through the full length. If it binds, find the high spot before loading it.
- Remove the shipping oil film with isopropyl alcohol, then apply a light machine oil. Keep oil away from the clamp contact surface.

### 4.3 End stops and bumpers

- Fit a stop at **both** rail ends, bolted to the spine just beyond the rail end, rated to take the monitor weight, not just stop the block. This prevents the block running off the rail and spilling the recirculating balls.
- Add rubber or felt bumpers to the top stop and bottom stop.
- The bottom bumper should be positioned so the monitor cannot hit the desk or any equipment below it.

### 4.4 Blocks and adapter plate

- Use **two blocks** about 100 mm apart (adjust for clamp body width) on the same rail, joined by the adapter plate. Two blocks resist the pitching load and reduce wobble when typing. A single block cantilevering the monitor will shake.
- MGN12H blocks use an **M3, 20 x 20 mm** hole pattern. A standard VESA bracket will not fit them.
- Drill the adapter plate (3-4 mm aluminium) for both blocks and for your monitor's VESA pattern (75 x 75 or 100 x 100, M4 usually).
- **Screw length limit:** plate thickness + block thread depth only. Check the datasheet; it is typically around 3.5 mm. A screw that bottoms out in the block will jam the carriage. Use thread-lock.
- If the monitor's VESA holes are recessed, add spacers and use longer VESA screws.

### 4.5 Pin lock (primary lock)

- Fit the M8 indexing plunger through the edge of the adapter plate, so its pin points toward the spine, beside the rail.
- Fix the 25 x 3 mm hole strip to the spine beside the rail and drill 8 mm holes at:
  - **"UP"** (monitor at the eye line), and
  - **"DOWN"** (monitor stowed).
- Position the holes so the pin engages when the plate is at the required height. Check before final drilling.
- To move the monitor: support it with one hand, pull the knob with the other, slide, and release into the next hole. Never release the pin without a hand on the monitor.

### 4.6 Clamp (secondary lock)

- Fit the clamp on the rail between or adjacent to the blocks and connect it to the adapter plate so it moves with the carriage.
- Keep oil off the clamp contact surface. Wipe the rail section it grips.
- Use it to stop rattle and drift. Do not rely on it alone to hold the monitor.

### 4.7 Safety strap

- Run a strap or cable from the adapter plate to a separate fixing at the top of the spine, rated ≥ 100 kg, with a little slack so it doesn't interfere with travel. If anything fails, the monitor hangs rather than falls.

### 4.8 Optional counterbalance

- A constant-force spring or gas strut sized to the monitor weight makes lifting one-handed and reduces drop energy.
- It must be matched to the monitor mass. Too strong and the monitor rises when unlocked; too weak and it drops.
- **Hazard:** if the monitor is removed while the spring is attached, the spring can retract violently. Always disconnect or restrain the spring before removing the monitor.
- Note that "no springs" is a design choice, not a safety advantage. A properly sized counterbalance is arguably safer than a heavy monitor held only by a pin.

